    <!-- Add Activity -->

<activity>
    <title>Discovering the Sine Rule Using String and Cardboard</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Work in groups of 3–4 to physically investigate the relationship between the sides of a triangle and the angles opposite to them. Through hands-on construction and measurement, you will discover and derive the Sine Rule.
        </p>

        <p>
            <alert>Learning Objective</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            Investigate the relationship between the sides of a triangle and their opposite angles and derive the Sine Rule through direct measurement and algebraic reasoning.
        </p>

        <p>
            <alert>Materials Needed</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
         <ul>
            <li><p>Manila paper or cardboard</p></li>
            <li><p>String (various lengths)</p></li>
            <li><p>Ruler (at least 30 cm)</p></li>
            <li><p>Protractor</p></li>
            <li><p>Scissors</p></li>
            <li><p>Marker pens</p></li>
            <li><p>Calculator</p></li>
            <li><p>Compass (for drawing circles)</p></li>
         </ul>
        </p>
          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Step 1: Construct a Triangle.</alert> In your group:
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(i)">
                    <li><p>Draw a large triangle ABC on the cardboard.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Label the sides and angles as follows:
                        <ul>
                            <li><m>AB = c</m> (opposite angle <m>C</m>)</li>
                            <li><m>BC = a</m> (opposite angle <m>A</m>)</li>
                            <li><m>AC = b</m> (opposite angle <m>B</m>)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p></li>
                    <li><p>Using a ruler, measure all three side lengths as accurately as possible.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Using a protractor, measure all three angles: <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Record all measurements in the table below.</p></li>
                </ol>
                </p>
                
                <table>
                    <title>Original Triangle Measurements</title>
                    <tabular halign="center">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                            <cell>Measurement</cell>
                            <cell>Value (cm or degrees)</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Side <m>a</m> (BC)</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Side <m>b</m> (AC)</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Side <m>c</m> (AB)</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Angle <m>A</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Angle <m>B</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Angle <m>C</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <note component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        Encourage students to measure multiple times and take an average for accuracy. The triangle should be drawn large (at least 15 cm on each side) to minimize measurement errors. Ensure they understand that in a triangle, each side is opposite to an angle.
                    </p>
                </note>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Step 2: Draw Perpendiculars from Each Vertex.</alert>
                </p>
                <ol marker="(i)">
                    <li><p>From vertex <m>A</m>, draw a perpendicular line to side <m>BC</m> (or the line containing <m>BC</m>). Label the foot of the perpendicular as <m>D</m>. This perpendicular height is <m>h_A</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>From vertex <m>B</m>, draw a perpendicular line to side <m>AC</m> (or the line containing <m>AC</m>). Label the foot of the perpendicular as <m>E</m>. This perpendicular height is <m>h_B</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>From vertex <m>C</m>, draw a perpendicular line to side <m>AB</m> (or the line containing <m>AB</m>). Label the foot of the perpendicular as <m>F</m>. This perpendicular height is <m>h_C</m>.</p></li>
                </ol>
                <note component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        Students will now have three different right-angled triangles formed. Emphasize that each perpendicular creates a right angle, which will allow us to use trigonometric ratios to express the heights.
                    </p>
                </note>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Step 3: Measure the Perpendicular Heights.</alert>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Carefully measure the three perpendicular heights on your cardboard diagram:
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li><p><m>h_A</m> = height from vertex <m>A</m> to side <m>BC</m></p></li>
                    <li><p><m>h_B</m> = height from vertex <m>B</m> to side <m>AC</m></p></li>
                    <li><p><m>h_C</m> = height from vertex <m>C</m> to side <m>AB</m></p></li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                    Record these measurements and then calculate what they should be using trigonometry:
                </p>
                <table>
                    <title>Perpendicular Heights: Measured vs. Calculated</title>
                    <tabular halign="center">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                            <cell>Height</cell>
                            <cell>Measured (cm)</cell>
                            <cell>Formula</cell>
                            <cell>Calculated (cm)</cell>
                            <cell>Difference</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>h_A</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell><m>b\sin C</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>h_B</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell><m>c\sin A</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>h_C</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell><m>a\sin B</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p>
                    <strong>Show your calculations:</strong>
                </p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>h_A = b\sin C = \underline{\hspace{2cm}} \times \sin(\underline{\hspace{1cm}}) = \underline{\hspace{2cm}}\text{ cm}</mrow>
                    <mrow>h_B = c\sin A = \underline{\hspace{2cm}} \times \sin(\underline{\hspace{1cm}}) = \underline{\hspace{2cm}}\text{ cm}</mrow>
                    <mrow>h_C = a\sin B = \underline{\hspace{2cm}} \times \sin(\underline{\hspace{1cm}}) = \underline{\hspace{2cm}}\text{ cm}</mrow>
                </md>
                <note component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        This step is crucial as it shows students that the height can be expressed in multiple ways depending on which angle and side we use. The key insight is that all three heights are parts of different right triangles within the main triangle.
                    </p>
                </note>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </statement>
</activity>

